<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Marsa Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Convention of La Marsa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The La Marsa Convention was an agreement signed on 8 June 1883 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Ali III ibn al-Husayn, bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. Supplementing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bardo">Treaty of Bardo</a> of 1881, it obliged the bey to carry out the administrative, judicial and financial reforms France considered useful, establishing a full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in which a French resident-general controlled government. France in turn guaranteed the Tunisian debt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-marsa-convention.jpg" alt="La Marsa Convention" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bey Husseinite of Tunis + French government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Premi%C3%A8re_page_de_la_convention_de_la_Marsa_-_Tunisie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
